<div class="page-head">
  <div class="page-head-row page-head-row--split page-head-row--stacked">
    <h1 class="section-title"><span class="gradient-text">About</span></h1>
    <%= link_to "Run Job", new_job_path, class: "btn btn-primary head-action" %>

  <p class="page-head-blurb">What this thing is, in plain terms. It traces a picture into vector candidates, draws or renders geometry, generates the sources it traces, and measures all of it against the rules a real screen-printer cares about. It never picks a winner — you choose, and your pick gets proved.</p>
  </div></div>

<div class="grid-2" style="align-items:stretch;">
  <div class="card">
    <h3>The pipeline</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      You give it a picture — a logo, a scan, an AI-generated image. It produces a set of
      traced vector candidates, measures each against the rules a real screen-printer cares
      about (colour count, node weight, stroke thickness, ink coverage), and shows you the
      trade-offs.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      It never picks a winner. Tracing quality is a visual judgment — you choose from the
      candidates and metrics, then your pick is proved: a proof image, a print PDF, and a manifest.
    </p>
    <div class="actions-row">
      <span class="tech-tag">prep → sweep → compare</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">you pick</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">proof · Layer A + Layer B</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>The two layers</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Layer A</strong> reads the SVG source:
      declared colours vs budget, palette conformance, node counts, stroke widths,
      banned embeds.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Layer B</strong> renders the file and
      measures the truth: real ink count after merging near-identical colours, total
      area coverage, gradient detection.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      Candidates sort by fewest hard gates failed, then fewest advisories — nothing more.
      Pixel fidelity (how close the trace renders to your original) is a second,
      independent lens.
    </p>
  </div>

</div>

<div class="card" data-about-stack>
  <h3>Runners · Jev · Flux</h3>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Runners.</strong>
    The GUI never runs the pipeline itself. It talks to a runner over HTTP, and a runner is
    a URL and a kind — <span class="mono">svg</span> is the real tracing pipeline,
    <span class="mono">flux</span> generates a raster to trace,
    <span class="mono">plot</span> draws a document and reports on runs,
    <span class="mono">blender</span> renders geometry in 3D, and
    <span class="mono">mock</span> simulates the whole thing locally for demos. The runner
    contract lives in <span class="mono">docs/runner-contract.md</span>. Tracing is VTracer
    only — never an LLM, never hand-drawn paths. Only an <span class="mono">svg</span>
    runner is a hand-off target: the other kinds trace nothing and hold no job state. The
    stack is four repos: <span class="mono">chopshop-svg</span> is the pipeline,
    <span class="mono">chopshop-jev</span> the decision sidecar,
    <span class="mono">chopshop-flux</span> generation, and
    <span class="mono">chopshop-bp</span> (Blend-Plot) the geometry and metrics service whose
    Plot half draws documents and whose Blender half renders 3D.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Jev · System One.</strong>
    After a proof, you can ask <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Jev</strong>
    (TypeSafe's System One model) four typed advisory questions: dominant issue, next action,
    evidence quality, and whether a human should eyeball the proof. It answers with typed
    decisions in a few hundred milliseconds — no prose, no hallucination surface. It never
    overrides the gates and never picks a candidate.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Flux · generation service.</strong>
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Flux</strong> generates the sources you trace,
    behind its own small standard API (<span class="mono">chopshop-flux</span>) — POST
    <span class="mono">/generate</span> and <span class="mono">/edit</span>, plus
    <span class="mono">/models</span>, <span class="mono">/health</span> and
    <span class="mono">/files/&lt;name&gt;</span> — replacing the old SwarmUI/ComfyUI
    integration. The registry is no longer one family: five checkpoints are registered
    (<%= FluxConf::MODELS.keys.join(" · ") %>) and every one of them loads. The default is
    <span class="mono"><%= FluxConf::DEFAULT_MODEL_KEY %></span>, Apache-2.0, so its output
    is yours to sell; <span class="mono">sdxl-1.0</span> is OpenRAIL++-M (sellable too) and
    is SD-style — it takes a real negative prompt and an img2img strength —
    <span class="mono">sd-1.5</span> is OpenRAIL-M, and
    <span class="mono">sdxl-turbo</span> is Stability AI Non-Commercial, so it only loads
    once <span class="mono">CHOPSHOP_FLUX_ACK_NONCOMMERCIAL=1</span> is set. Two rules keep
    it opt-in: this layer calls the pipeline, and the pipeline never calls it; and it never
    judges the art — it produces sources, a human picks. The settings and the
    model registry live on the <%= link_to "FLUX page", flux_path %>, which mirrors
    <span class="mono">chopshop-flux</span>'s <span class="mono">config.py</span> and
    <span class="mono">models.py</span>.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h3>What actually happens when you press Run</h3>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    This is the tracing story, and it is the long one. A job filed against a
    <span class="mono">flux</span> or <span class="mono">bp</span> runner does one
    thing instead of six — it makes a source, or draws or renders the one document
    that IS the deliverable — and the operations below are what those two talk to.
    (A job filed against a legacy <span class="mono">plot</span> or
    <span class="mono">blender</span> label still runs: those kinds were folded into
    <span class="mono">bp</span> rather than removed, so nothing already filed broke.)
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    The studio does none of the work itself. It uploads your image, writes a job
    record, and hands the runner a spec. Everything below happens on the runner; this
    page just polls for the result.
  </p>
  <ol style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;padding-left:1.2rem;">
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Upload</strong> — PNG or JPEG, ≤ 50MB,
      magic-byte checked. The studio never decodes it; the bytes are proxied straight
      through to the runner.</li>
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Prep</strong> — the raster is made
      pipeline-legal: format and colour-mode checks, aspect ratio reported (never
      silently stretched), optional background removal and upscale. Prep is
      <em>check-first</em>: it reports what it would change and only rewrites when
      asked. It also writes a <span class="mono">colour-inverted twin</span> of your
      source, so you can compare how a design reads on black.</li>
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Sweep</strong> — VTracer runs the same
      image under many parameter sets (presets, speckle filtering, palette depth,
      node-preservation modes), producing a dozen candidate SVGs. Two of the levers
      are genuinely orthogonal: one moves the <em>colour count</em>, the other moves the
      <em>worst single path's node count</em>. Neither alone gets a file under the
      node budget, so the interesting configurations are the 2-D ones.</li>
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Compare</strong> — every candidate is
      scored on the gates, and (optionally) rendered back to a raster and diffed
      against your original. That fidelity number is measured over <em>artwork</em>
      pixels only — an overall average is dominated by the background and flatters
      bad traces.</li>
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">You pick</strong> — from the table,
      the preview tiles, and the metrics. Nothing advances on its own.</li>
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Validate</strong> — Layer A + Layer B
      on your pick, producing a proof PNG, <span class="mono">print.pdf</span>, and a
      manifest recording every tool version and measurement.</li>
  </ol>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    A job moves through
    <span class="mono"><%= Job::STATES.join(" → ") %></span>.
    While it is <%= Job::IN_FLIGHT_STATES.join(" / ") %> the page polls the runner for
    a log tail; you can cancel a long run, and a finished job can be deleted from the
    list. The runner also keeps a disk-capacity gate, so a run that would fill the
    volume stops itself rather than wedging the box.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="card" data-about-steps>
  <h3>Sequences — several actions in one job</h3>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    A job is normally ONE action: pick a runner, give it what it needs, run it. Ticking
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Sequence</strong> on the Run Job page makes
    it an ORDERED LIST of actions instead — a generation, a trace, a render, in the order
    you put them — and the whole list is still one job, one queue slot, one record.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    Each step names the runner it runs on and carries that runner's own request, which is
    the same block the panel above composes: the sequence rides in the job's contract under
    <span class="mono">steps</span>, so a hand-written contract still wins and there is one
    place a document is defined rather than a second copy to keep in step.
  </p>
  <h4 style="color:var(--text-primary);margin-top:1rem;">The studio drives the chain, not the runner</h4>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);">
    A runner here is stateless and serves one operation per request — it holds no idea that
    a chain exists. So the studio posts step one, waits for it to finish, then posts step
    two with step one's output handed in, and so on. That is why a sequence needs no runner
    change at all, and why the order in the list IS the order they run.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    The hand-off is where a chain earns its keep: a step that cannot run without a source
    takes the file the step before it produced, when that is a file it accepts. A render
    step handed a drawing has that drawing converted into the render's own geometry
    vocabulary, because the render operation takes geometry rather than a file path. Where a
    hand-off cannot be worked out, the sequence is refused and says which step it could not
    resolve, rather than running something with no input.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    If a step fails the chain stops at that step and the job says which one it was; the
    steps already finished keep their output. Cancelling stops it between steps. Progress is
    recorded per step, so a job interrupted and picked up again reports where it actually got
    to rather than claiming the whole list ran.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    Untick the box and the job is exactly the single action it always was: a contract with
    no <span class="mono">steps</span> key behaves the same as it did before this existed.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="card" data-about-bp>
  <h3>BP · Blend-Plot — one service with two halves</h3>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">BP</strong> is
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Blend-Plot</strong>: ONE program that draws
    documents and renders geometry in 3D. Its repo was renamed
    <span class="mono">chopshop-plot</span> → <span class="mono">chopshop-bp</span> precisely
    because it hosts both halves, so it gets ONE page
    (<%= link_to "BP", bp_path %>) with a Plot section and a Blender section rather than two
    pages pretending it is two services.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Plot</strong> is the document half: one JSON
    document in, one SVG out — the geometry spec, the metric charts and the run records, plus
    the hierarchy and the event log the STATUS page draws.
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Blender</strong> is the 3D half: a geometry
    document in, rendered with headless Blender — the frame itself, or a manifest of frames
    plus an optional GLB. Same port, same token, one service.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    The studio still files them under <em>two runner kinds</em>
    (<span class="mono">plot</span> and <span class="mono">blender</span>), because a deployment
    may run one half without the other and neither is a hand-off target. The old pages are gone:
    <span class="mono">/plot</span> and <span class="mono">/blender</span> redirect to the one
    page, so an old link still lands somewhere.
  </p>
  <div class="actions-row">
    <span class="tech-tag">one service</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">plot draws documents</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">blend renders 3D</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">one page</span>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="card" data-about-ownership>
  <h3>Three jobs, three owners — svg computes, studio presents, bp draws</h3>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">chopshop-svg</strong> (the pipeline)
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">computes the machine learning</strong> — the
    learned ranker, the bucket bandit and the triage gates — and is the source of truth for
    those constants. <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">chopshop-studio</strong> (this
    app) <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">owns the metrics engine</strong>: the
    configuration of that ML and the presentation of server statistics — the footer's
    check-engine light, the STATUS page it leads to, and
    <span class="mono">GET /engine.json</span>. The studio does not compute the ML; it records
    the signal the pipeline trains on, reports the shipped constants verbatim, and serves the
    counts. <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">chopshop-bp</strong> is the service the
    studio <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">calls</strong> for blend-plot work —
    drawing documents and rendering 3D: it applies no scoring, keeps no telemetry, and never
    picks a winner. Drawing a document is itself a blend-plot task, so the STATUS page's
    hierarchy and event timeline being drawn by bp is the intended division of labour, not a
    computation that belongs here.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    One thing to read carefully: <span class="mono">metrics_version: 1</span> is a shared flat
    <em>data contract</em> — the shape of the run records the chart operations read — not a
    scoring engine. Nothing on this page or in bp ranks, scores or recommends.
  </p>
  <div class="actions-row">
    <span class="tech-tag">svg computes the ML</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">studio owns the metrics engine</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">bp draws and renders</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">bp scores nothing</span>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="card" data-about-plot>
  <h3>BP · the Plot half — five operations, one JSON contract</h3>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Plot</strong> is the document half of BP: one JSON
    document in, one SVG out, and it draws nothing from your raster. Every operation takes one
    JSON document and answers with one SVG — or refuses with a JSON envelope naming what was
    wrong, rather than drawing something else. Five operations ship:
  </p>
  <ul style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;padding-left:1.2rem;">
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">generate</strong> — a geometry spec
      (seven element types — <span class="mono">rect, circle, ellipse, polygon, polyline,
      line, path</span> — on a canvas, optionally tiled) in, an SVG out. That SVG is a
      programmatic <em>source</em>: the pipeline can consume it as a normal candidate.</li>
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">digest</strong> — one run's numbers as
      a bar, line or scatter chart. Auto-scaled, so a chart is never clipped.</li>
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">query</strong> — run records plus a
      two-field chart spec: a cross-run scatter, a Pareto trade-off frontier, or a
      two-way diff of two candidates or two recipes.</li>
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">tree</strong> — nodes and edges as a
      hierarchical blueprint, laid out left-to-right. This is what the STATUS page's
      source → job → candidate hierarchy is drawn with.</li>
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">log</strong> — events as a vertical
      timeline, one row per recorded action. This is how STATUS draws the event log.</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">The axis is always your choice.</strong>
    No chart hardcodes a field, nothing ranks the metric fields or recommends one, and
    the Pareto chart <em>highlights</em> the non-dominated frontier rather than
    collapsing it to a winner — it never picks a winner either. Every chart is drawn
    from one flat, versioned metrics row per candidate, and a measurement that was
    skipped is absent rather than zero, because a chart that plots 0 for a missing
    measurement is lying.
  </p>
  <div class="actions-row">
    <span class="tech-tag">generate</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">digest</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">query</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">tree</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">log</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">the axis is yours</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">missing is a state, not a zero</span>
  </div>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    The settings, the chart shapes and the metric field registry are on the
    <%= link_to "BP page", bp_path %>, in its Plot section, which mirrors
    <span class="mono">chopshop-bp</span>'s
    <span class="mono">config.py</span> and <span class="mono">metrics.py</span>.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="card" data-about-blender>
  <h3>BP · the Blender half — geometry you can walk around</h3>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Blender</strong> renders a geometry document
    with headless Blender. It is the 3D half of the same service as Plot — the same port, the
    same token, one more route — but its own runner <em>kind</em>, because rasterising
    geometry in 3D is a different job target from drawing a 2D document, and a
    deployment may run one without the other.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    A document is an <span class="mono">elements</span> list plus optional blocks.
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Extrude</strong> is what makes it 3D:
    a closed contour (<span class="mono">rect</span>, <span class="mono">ellipse</span>,
    <span class="mono">polygon</span>, or a closed <span class="mono">path</span>)
    becomes a filled, bevelled solid, while an open element —
    <span class="mono">line</span>, <span class="mono">polyline</span>, an open
    <span class="mono">path</span> — renders as a stroke tube whose radius is its
    <span class="mono">stroke_width</span>. The render block sets the frame
    (default <span class="mono">1024 × 1024</span>), the background, the view count,
    and the engine: <span class="mono">workbench</span>, the fast rasteriser that needs
    no GPU, or <span class="mono">eevee</span>, the shaded one.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Views decide the response shape.</strong>
    One view with no export answers the rendered frame itself, as
    <span class="mono">image/png</span> — one round trip, and the studio stores the
    bytes. More than one view, or a GLB export, answers a JSON manifest naming the
    frames on the runner, and the client fetches each one from
    <span class="mono">GET /files/&lt;name&gt;</span>. A runner without Blender
    installed answers <span class="mono">503</span>: that is a state a deployment is
    allowed to be in, not a fault, and the job fails with the service's own reason.
  </p>
  <div class="actions-row">
    <span class="tech-tag">closed → solid</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">open → tube</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">1 view = the frame</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">n views = a manifest</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">glb optional, off by default</span>
  </div>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    The document's vocabulary and the two response shapes are in the
    <%= link_to "BP page", bp_path %>'s Blender section.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="grid-2" style="align-items:stretch;margin-top:1rem;">
  <div class="card">
    <h3>The Scrapbook</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      A drawer down the left edge of every page, and the operator's own store of things
      worth keeping. Star a source raster, its inverted twin, a candidate or a proof
      anywhere it appears and it lands here. Generated rasters — a
      <span class="mono">flux</span> variation — and flattened composites land here too.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">A copy, not a reference.</strong>
      The bytes are copied into the scrapbook's own storage, so deleting the job they
      came from leaves the scrapbook intact. That is the whole requirement, and it is why
      the item carries its own <span class="mono">sha256</span>: content is the
      identity, so the same bytes saved twice are one item rather than two.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      Every item can be <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">sent back to Run
      Job</strong> — the scrapbook copy is handed to the new-job form as the artwork
      (the same contents, ready to trace again), or to FLUX as a reference image if it
      is a raster.
    </p>
    <div class="actions-row">
      <span class="tech-tag">survives the job</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">copy, not a reference</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">back to Run Job</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>The Composite builder</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      Assemble a source raster out of layered pieces on a canvas. A layer is not a file:
      it references where its pixels came from — a scrapbook item, an upload, a blank
      slate — plus the placement that makes it a component (x/y/w/h, opacity, hue,
      stacking order). The parts stay editable for as long as the composite exists.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Flatten is the one server-side step.</strong>
      The browser composes the pixels for the preview, but the runner only reads uploads,
      and a canvas is not a file — so the composed PNG is posted to the server, which
      stores it twice: once as a scrapbook item (the re-usable artefact) and once as the
      upload the job will use. It is routed through the scrapbook deliberately: one store
      for "things I made" is simpler than two.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      "Run it" then hands that upload to the new-job form. The default canvas is
      <span class="mono">3000 × 3000</span>, transparent.
    </p>
    <div class="actions-row">
      <span class="tech-tag">layers, not files</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">flatten on the server</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">via the scrapbook</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="card" data-about-ranking>
  <h3>The ranking signal — gates, log, ranker, bandit</h3>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    The order candidates are shown in is not a mystery, and it is not a black box that
    chooses for you. Five things make it up, and every one of them is owner-scoped: one
    operator's actions are never another operator's training data.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">P1 · hard gates.</strong>
    Thresholds with shipped defaults —
    <span class="mono">max_unique_colors 8 · max_speckles 40 · max_glow_area_pct 8 ·
    min_silhouette_iou 0.85 · min_luma_delta 12 · weak_needed 3 · bucket_cap 2</span>.
    A <em>hard</em> gate drops a candidate on any hit; a <em>weak</em> gate demotes it
    once the <span class="mono">weak_needed</span> hits accumulate. No verdict deletes
    anything — a drop is a discard, and the human can still act on it.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">P2 · the append-only event log.</strong>
    Every star, unstar, pick and delete is a row, carrying the artifact's checksum, the
    position it held in the ordered list, and which ranker proposed that order. Positions
    are 1-based on both sides, so exposure and action join on one scale. The log has no
    foreign key to jobs — a row is provenance, not a child — so the signal outlives the
    job it describes.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">P3 · text similarity.</strong>
    A pure-Python bigram/TF-IDF cosine over prompts, filenames, tags and finding text,
    kept deterministic and digit-preserving, so two parameter sets that differ only in a
    number are not read as the same thing.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">P4 · the learned pairwise ranker.</strong>
    It learns which candidate a human preferred, from within-job pairs, and only once the
    log holds <span class="mono">learn_min_picks=30</span> picks across
    <span class="mono">learn_min_jobs=2</span> jobs. Below either threshold the
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">hand order</strong> stands — fewest hard
    gates, then advisories, then artwork MAE, with a candidate that is missing a reading
    sorted last. Its features include the gate counts, node count, artwork MAE, colour
    and speckle counts, bucketed bins and a one-hot per preset. And it is deliberately
    kept honest: 1 slot of the visible top-8 is held back for a random or uncertain
    candidate (<span class="mono">n_random_slots=1</span>, <span class="mono">window=8</span>),
    so the order a human acts on is never fed back as its own answer.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">P5 · the bucket bandit.</strong>
    Beta-Bernoulli Thompson sampling decides which bucket to try next — over bucket keys,
    never over candidates. One draw in ten explores a bucket the statistics do not favour
    (<span class="mono">epsilon 0.1</span>), bucket statistics expire after 30 days so the
    mix follows the sources in front of you, and a veto list removes a bucket outright
    whatever its sampled score.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">It never picks a winner.</strong> The ranker
    and the bandit <em>order</em> and <em>propose</em>; the human picks. The learned files
    (<span class="mono">learned_ranker.json</span>, <span class="mono">learned_buckets.json</span>)
    live in the pipeline's learning dir, because the pipeline trains — this studio records
    the signal and serves it at <span class="mono">GET /events</span>.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    The recorded signal in counts, the log and the hierarchy drawn from it, and this whole
    configuration in full are on the <%= link_to "STATUS page", status_path %> — reached
    from the footer's check-engine light, which is amber until there is any signal to
    learn from and green once there is.
  </p>
  <div class="actions-row">
    <span class="tech-tag">P1 gates</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">P2 event log</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">P3 text similarity</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">P4 pairwise ranker</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">P5 bandit</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">it never picks a winner</span>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="grid-2" style="align-items:stretch;margin-top:1rem;">
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Reading the jobs list</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      The jobs index has two views of the same runs, and a toggle for them.
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">List</strong> is the table — runner and
      passed-count columns, one row per run. <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Grid</strong>
      is the same runs as thumbnail cards: one picture, the name, the state, the passed
      count, and the actions. The grid is deliberately low-information, so a card stays
      a picture you recognise rather than a paragraph. The choice is remembered in the
      browser.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      The card actions are icons: <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">preview</strong>
      (the same popup the job page opens), <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">download</strong>
      (the finished run's <span class="mono">7z</span> archive),
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">send to Run Job</strong> (reuse the source
      raster), <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">save to the scrapbook</strong>,
      and <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">cancel or delete</strong> — cancel
      while a run is in flight, delete once it is not.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      They are inline SVG rather than text glyphs on purpose: the pixel font does not
      contain them, so a glyph silently falls back to another face and draws at a
      different size inside the same box.
    </p>
    <div class="actions-row">
      <span class="tech-tag">List / Grid</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">icon actions</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">remembered</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Node weight, and why a trace can still be too heavy</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      A cutter has a limit on how many points a single path may carry. A trace can
      clear every colour and ink rule and still be unprintable purely because one path
      has too many nodes. That case is remediable, but only by simplifying that
      specific path — rewriting the whole file makes things measurably worse.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      Simplification is easy to do badly. Every failure mode found while building it
      looked like a <em>success</em>: an unsupported curve quietly deleted a shape, two
      separate shapes quietly welded into one, a closed path quietly lost its fill
      closure. All of them still reported a lower node count. So the tool refuses to
      accept a fit it cannot verify, and reports what it declined.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      The number to watch is not the node count but the
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">rendered result</strong>: the same file
      can clear its budget while visibly eroding. Proof image, not gate, is the pass
      condition.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>How this stays private</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      Each user signs in with a password — their password IS their identity, and
      it gates access to their own jobs, candidates, proofs, scrapbook and
      composites; no one sees anyone else's work. The same scoping covers the
      learning signal: the event log, the telemetry counts and the model trained on
      them are per-operator, so one person's choices are never another's training data.
      Failed attempts are logged with timestamps and the reference deployment runs
      <span class="mono">fail2ban</span> against that log. Runners are never bound
      to a public interface.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      Search engines are declined twice over:
      <span class="mono">robots.txt</span> disallows everything, and every response
      carries <span class="mono">X-Robots-Tag: noindex</span>. The password is the
      actual protection — the other two are a request, and a crawler that ignores
      robots.txt will ignore this too.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Reading a comparison table</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">hard</strong> — a rule the cutter will
      not forgive (too many colours, banned embed, a physical-size violation). A hard
      failure is a stop.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">advisory</strong> — worth knowing, not
      fatal. Treat these as the shortlist filter.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">MAE_art</strong> — mean pixel error over
      artwork pixels only. Lower is closer to your original. It is a
      <em>measurement</em>, and two candidates can tie on it while looking completely
      different. It ranks rows; it does not choose.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">PALETTE</strong> — the trace's real
      colours, which frequently are not the ones you asked for. Read this before
      trusting any colour-sorted column.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h3>Things it will not do</h3>
  <ul style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;padding-left:1.2rem;">
    <li>Pick a candidate for you, or advance one to a proof on its own — not the studio,
      not the ranker, not the bandit.</li>
    <li>Trace, validate, render, or generate anything itself — that is the runner's job,
      and the boundary is deliberate.</li>
    <li>Generate or edit SVG paths with an LLM.</li>
    <li>Hardcode a chart axis, rank the metric fields, or plot a missing measurement as a
      zero.</li>
    <li>Stretch artwork to fit a spec. An aspect mismatch is reported, never
      "fixed" by distortion.</li>
    <li>Prescribe a print size. Add dimensions to a spec only when the job has a
      fixed physical size.</li>
    <li>Let one operator's actions train on another's work.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
